    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Execution / Defense Evasion (Masqueraded Payload Launch)", "technique": "T1036.005 + T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-10-03 14:08:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-EXEC-12 (10.30.7.42) logged image=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe, parentImage=C:\\Windows\\explorer.exe, user=CORP\\nadia.r, signed=false, hash=d8e9c2a1b3f7c6d5a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1. The binary name 'svchost32.exe' is a deliberate masquerade — it impersonates the legitimate Windows Service Host (real path C:\\Windows\\System32\\svchost.exe) but is dropped in a USER-WRITABLE AppData\\Roaming path (no admin required) under an EXECUTIVE account. Parent=explorer.exe indicates user-double-click execution path (likely launched via a phishing-attachment or persistence-shortcut clickthrough, NOT auto-launched by a service). signed=false confirms the binary is not Authenticode-signed. This is the canonical user-space masqueraded-loader signature.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with image matching '*\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost*.exe' (svchost should NEVER live in a user profile path) + signed=false + parentImage=explorer.exe is a categorical T1036.005 masquerading indicator. Executive-user context elevates business impact.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:08:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence Verification (Post-Launch)", "technique": "T1036.005", "what_happened": "At 14:11:02 (2 minutes 20 seconds after initial launch), a second Sysmon Event 1 fired for the SAME image=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe under user=CORP\\nadia.r. This is the post-launch self-verification or relaunch pattern often produced by stagers that fork a child capture-worker after a brief initialization delay (often used to set up command-and-control channel handshake or load capture configuration before the active phase begins).", "chain_reason": "Repeated Sysmon Event 1 with identical image path under the same user within minutes is consistent with stager → worker handoff, NOT legitimate single-instance application launch.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:11:02+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection (Screen Capture API Invocation — SMOKING GUN)", "technique": "T1113", "what_happened": "At 14:11:05 (3 seconds after the post-launch event), the EDR API_CALL sensor fired on WS-EXEC-12: process=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe invoked the Windows GDI screen-copy API 'BitBlt' (gdi32.dll) AND 'CreateCompatibleDC' (creates a memory device context compatible with the screen). The BitBlt + CreateCompatibleDC pairing is THE definitive Windows GDI screen-capture fingerprint: CreateCompatibleDC + GetDC(NULL) + BitBlt(SRCCOPY) is the canonical 4-call sequence used by every Windows screen-capture tool from legitimate tools (mspaint Snip) to malicious tools (RedLine / FormBook / NjRAT / custom spyware). When called from an unsigned user-space binary that has NO legitimate screen-capture business reason, this is T1113 Screen Capture confirmed.", "chain_reason": "EDR API_CALL telemetry: process=svchost32.exe in AppData\\Roaming calling BitBlt + CreateCompatibleDC from gdi32.dll. Unsigned non-Microsoft caller of GDI screen-copy APIs is a categorical T1113 indicator.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:11:05+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Collection (Screenshot File Write #1)", "technique": "T1113 + T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 14:11:06 (1 second after the BitBlt call), Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate): targetFilename=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\scr_141106.jpg. The screenshot is being written to the local staging directory. The filename pattern scr_HHMMSS.jpg is a tool default — the timestamp suffix matches the capture time exactly, indicating the tool names every capture by wall-clock to facilitate later periodic-batch exfiltration. The .jpg location in Temp is the canonical 'staging file before exfiltration' pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 with targetFilename in %Temp% matching scr_*.jpg appearing within seconds of a BitBlt API call from the same process is the staging-file confirmation. scr_HHMMSS.jpg naming is a tool default.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:11:06+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Collection (Screenshot File Write #2 — Periodic Cadence)", "technique": "T1113 + T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 14:11:36 (EXACTLY 30 seconds after scr_141106.jpg), Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\scr_141136.jpg. The 30-second precision interval confirms a PERIODIC capture timer — the tool is configured to snap the screen every 30s and dump to Temp. Over an 8-hour workday this produces ~960 screenshots = a near-video reconstruction of everything the executive sees, including emails, board materials, M&A documents, password-manager-vault unlocks, banking sessions, and chat content.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 second capture with timestamp delta of EXACTLY 30 seconds proves a fixed-interval timer (no jitter). Periodic screen capture against an executive endpoint is the canonical T1113 + T1074.001 staging pattern.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:11:36+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Collection (Screenshot File Write #3 — Cadence Confirmed)", "technique": "T1113 + T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 14:12:06 (again 30 seconds after the previous capture), Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\scr_141206.jpg. The third consecutive 30-second-delta capture eliminates any doubt about the cadence — this is a programmed periodic screen-surveillance routine, not a one-off snapshot.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 third capture, again with timestamp delta of EXACTLY 30 seconds, definitively confirms the periodic-timer routine.", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:12:06+0200"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Command & Control + Exfiltration (Batch Upload)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1041", "what_happened": "At 14:12:10 (4 seconds after the third capture), Sysmon Event 3: image=C:\\Users\\nadia.r\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe initiated outbound HTTPS POST to destinationIp=45.79.122.18 destinationPort=443. The 4-second gap after the third screenshot indicates the tool is on a 3-capture-then-exfil batching pattern (capture 3 screenshots over 90 seconds, then POST the bundle to C2). The destination 45.79.122.18 is a known C&C+Hacking node hosted in the US via Linode. Each batch POST contains the accumulated JPGs (likely concatenated or zipped before upload).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=svchost32.exe + destinationIp external + close temporal proximity to the third screenshot file = batch-exfil pattern. T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: HTTPS) + T1041 (Exfiltration Over C2 Channel).", "first_seen": "2026-10-03T14:12:10+0200"}
